WEBVTT

1
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.910
Art A: Well, hello, Tim.

2
00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.600
Tim Nunamaker: Oh, hey Art.

3
00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:24.710
Art A: Might just be you and me.

4
00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:25.820
Tim Nunamaker: It might just.

5
00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:28.739
Art A: That's all good.

6
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.300
Tim Nunamaker: Said he might join.

7
00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:31.540
Art A: Who?

8
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.010
Tim Nunamaker: I think Vlad said he might jump in.

9
00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:42.710
Art A: Okay, no worries. I think to just go through some of the changes, because this is all going to be recorded on YouTube, it's a bad time, I think. Like, in APAC, this is, like.

10
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:46.949
Art A: 7am Singapore time, so it's not a great time.

11
00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:52.069
Tim Nunamaker: Cool. Yeah, that makes sense. I'll just jump into it.

12
00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:57.369
Tim Nunamaker: I'm just going to drop a link to the engineers in case they do want to join.

13
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:03.730
Art A: I'll be back in two seconds.

14
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.820
Tim Nunamaker: If you are watching from the recording, thank you for joining us.

15
00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.349
Tim Nunamaker: I lost my screen share. Here we go.

16
00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:19.749
Tim Nunamaker: So I'm just gonna jump in.

17
00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:24.100
Tim Nunamaker: This is the formal review update for PDPP.

18
00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.060
Tim Nunamaker: mostly through September, we held a

19
00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:41.149
Tim Nunamaker: comment period on the first draft of v0.1. And I'm going to be going through some of the changes that we made to the protocol. They're still all available for comment.

20
00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:46.869
Tim Nunamaker: So, we've responded to a lot of the feedback and made some other changes, and those

21
00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.650
Tim Nunamaker: Those changes are also available for comment.

22
00:04:50.200 --> 00:05:06.650
Tim Nunamaker: In the last session, we talked about, governance and conformance. That was led by Art. There should be a recording and some notes if you want to check that out as well. And then we've… because we've had so much great feedback, and there are so many changes, we are

23
00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:14.280
Tim Nunamaker: aiming for the end of October, or when we will lock the first version of the protocol.

24
00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:18.909
Tim Nunamaker: And shortly after that, the governance program will go live.

25
00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:26.939
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so currently, every change that we're making that I'm going to talk about today

26
00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:29.879
Tim Nunamaker: is a pull request in GitHub.

27
00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:41.209
Tim Nunamaker: And you can look through PR number 374 as a good entry point. It links to all of the other changes and gives a short description of each change.

28
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:51.160
Tim Nunamaker: The changes are open for about a week, so that contributors can comment, maintainers have time to review, and then

29
00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:57.279
Tim Nunamaker: We hope to ship most of these, if not all of them, Over the next few weeks.

30
00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:04.300
Tim Nunamaker: Most of the changes fall into One of three categories.

31
00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.680
Tim Nunamaker: The first one is really about.

32
00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:10.039
Tim Nunamaker: Taking a close look at.

33
00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.750
Tim Nunamaker: Personal data owners and what consent they actually want to give.

34
00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:22.619
Tim Nunamaker: Another one is… Exactly when… when apps request

35
00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:31.470
Tim Nunamaker: Data, or query into data, making sure that they understand what they're getting and what they're working with, so that it's properly useful to them.

36
00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:49.720
Tim Nunamaker: And we understand that we can't solve every problem in this first version of the spec, and so we're carving out places and making space for the protocol to grow in a healthy way over time, and for profiles to be created that hook in on top of the core spec.

37
00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:55.539
Tim Nunamaker: So, there are about a dozen changes that I'll run through. Some of them will be…

38
00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:00.430
Tim Nunamaker: probably more interesting than others. Some of them are basically bug fixes, for lack of a better word.

39
00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:07.830
Tim Nunamaker: But the first one is about making sure that the user can

40
00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:14.250
Tim Nunamaker: Narrow consent. If what an app is requesting is more than they want to share.

41
00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:18.820
Tim Nunamaker: So, I may have required fields as a client application.

42
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:26.000
Tim Nunamaker: And I need those to be provided by the user in order for me to fulfill some, you know, useful purpose for that user.

43
00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:32.390
Tim Nunamaker: But even in that scenario, the user may still be uncomfortable with sharing, so they can indicate that

44
00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:51.439
Tim Nunamaker: they do not, want to grant access to all of the data that I'm requesting access to, maybe a subset of that. That could cause the grant to be rejected, but then as the client, I would see that perhaps I could make a different request or communicate some messaging to the user and try again.

45
00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:53.250
Tim Nunamaker: And…

46
00:07:53.560 --> 00:08:01.749
Tim Nunamaker: Just bundled into this is a little bit of a bug fix to ensure that clients are very explicit when they're asking for

47
00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:09.549
Tim Nunamaker: more than a specific set of fields. If you want to grant access… if you want access to all fields, then you need to indicate that.

48
00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:15.319
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so moving on to the next change.

49
00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:17.950
Tim Nunamaker: Every grant

50
00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:28.039
Tim Nunamaker: Could actually have multiple purposes. So, for example, if a music application wants your listing history to give you recommendations.

51
00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:32.409
Tim Nunamaker: and also separately wants to do some AI training on your data.

52
00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:38.580
Tim Nunamaker: Those are different purposes, and even if it's one UI to the user and one consent flow.

53
00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:43.580
Tim Nunamaker: There could be… Different grants that are created.

54
00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:55.599
Tim Nunamaker: And maybe they're configured slightly differently by the user, and maybe later the user decides to revoke one of them, but not both of them. And so this is to better align with GDPR, and just to give the user a little bit more control.

55
00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:11.360
Tim Nunamaker: Moving on to another couple of pull requests. We were thinking about use cases where the subject of the data, or the owner of the data, may be different from the person who's granting access to the data.

56
00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:25.129
Tim Nunamaker: And so we created something called a grantor, and grants can contain grantors, which is just a list of who is actually providing The consent here, if it's different from the subject.

57
00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:28.549
Tim Nunamaker: And then how you confirm.

58
00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:42.770
Tim Nunamaker: The authority of that person to share the data is a little bit of a separate question, and this leads into a larger topic around how do you provide verifiable evidence of

59
00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:54.560
Tim Nunamaker: the provenance of data, or the authority of somebody over data, etc. And I'll talk about that a little bit more with some other changes, but the short version is.

60
00:09:54.730 --> 00:10:11.539
Tim Nunamaker: We are trying to create, like, an envelope where evidence that can be verified can go into the grant, and then… and into the records of the data, and then how that's verified is a little bit, it's left

61
00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:18.709
Tim Nunamaker: up to certain profiles. The protocol isn't supposed… isn't meant to dictate exactly how that should work.

62
00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:21.360
Tim Nunamaker: Or at least there should be different options.

63
00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:37.990
Art A: Tim, just a quick question on that one, because I think this relates to a potential live implementation that we have, where we need mothers to be able to grant access to their children's medical records for the purposes of administering proactive healthcare.

64
00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:40.889
Art A: Like, obviously, like.

65
00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:45.839
Art A: Like, how do you get, or is the protocol silent on it, but like basically like.

66
00:10:47.040 --> 00:11:02.990
Art A: a child can't, especially, like, if it's a newborn child, can't be like, hey, mom, consent, and, like, click a button on a screen. Like, how would that work within this framework of the mother being the grantor? Are there default grantors, or are there any rules like that that we should set up?

67
00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:12.830
Tim Nunamaker: At this stage, there are no rules about Like, how would you map the legal…

68
00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:16.909
Tim Nunamaker: Power of attorney of someone within a certain jurisdiction into.

69
00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:22.069
Tim Nunamaker: a PDPP conformant server. But the protocol basically says.

70
00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:31.779
Tim Nunamaker: If you have some way to express, this is the grantor, and this is their relationship to the subject, and

71
00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:34.730
Tim Nunamaker: Here is verifiable evidence.

72
00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:38.369
Tim Nunamaker: There's a place for that evidence to be attached.

73
00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:42.150
Tim Nunamaker: And then… In order to actually facilitate.

74
00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:46.019
Tim Nunamaker: The verification process, like, defining that mechanism.

75
00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:51.189
Tim Nunamaker: we… I think we would need another profile, like a separate

76
00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:59.499
Tim Nunamaker: small specification to say, okay, we're going to use DIDs, or we're going to use, you know, a ZK-based

77
00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:00.400
Tim Nunamaker: Proof.

78
00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:06.290
Tim Nunamaker: Here's exactly how that verification technology can be used in this context.

79
00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:10.790
Tim Nunamaker: And so, the protocol… Probably can't solve for all of those.

80
00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:15.049
Tim Nunamaker: Right away, but… Like, the very first…

81
00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:33.790
Tim Nunamaker: implementation, or the very first, sort of trial of that, can set out some profile saying, hey, we're going to use a DID for this, and here's the kind of data that we're working with for evidence, and here's how the mechanism will work. And I think subsequent profiles for different

82
00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:36.890
Tim Nunamaker: technologies or different domains.

83
00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:42.370
Tim Nunamaker: Could probably sort of build on the previous ones. That's the current thinking.

84
00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.670
Art A: Okay, cool, cool, cool.

85
00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:46.340
Art A: Thank you.

86
00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:58.980
Tim Nunamaker: So moving forward, this one is pretty straightforward. If a data source has consent text defined within it, like within its schema.

87
00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:14.699
Tim Nunamaker: and that happens to be, let's say, in English, but not all users for… who are relevant for that data source are English-speaking, there's just a place now where the data source maintainer can define, here's the same language, but in Spanish.

88
00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:27.219
Tim Nunamaker: And because this is specific to the data source, this is going to go into the source declaration that defines the data source from the perspective of the server, the resource server that's serving that data.

89
00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:31.259
Tim Nunamaker: But basically, this is unopinionated.

90
00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:37.400
Tim Nunamaker: or it's not original invention by us, we're using language tags that follow the IETF.

91
00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:45.910
Tim Nunamaker: standard for how language tags should be defined, and then there's an RFC4647, which basically

92
00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:55.520
Tim Nunamaker: says that servers can use users' preferred language tags. So there shouldn't be anything controversial here, but we just wanted to make sure that this wasn't a gap.

93
00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:04.910
Tim Nunamaker: And now, moving on to the next change, We recognized, while thinking about

94
00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:18.710
Tim Nunamaker: Sort of as more data sources are included over time into PVP servers, there may be cases where you might have two instances of the same data source, or the same individual.

95
00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:21.779
Tim Nunamaker: And… We just want to make sure that.

96
00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:27.679
Tim Nunamaker: When that happens, the specific instance is linked to the record.

97
00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:33.260
Tim Nunamaker: And… So that's the first piece of this. And then the next piece of this is.

98
00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:41.380
Tim Nunamaker: more of a privacy fix. So, if… Two different applications separately request

99
00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:50.360
Tim Nunamaker: data for an individual, they shouldn't be able to compare the IDs that they get in that data and say, oh, we've matched on the ID, so we can

100
00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:53.110
Tim Nunamaker: We can assume that this is the same person.

101
00:14:53.600 --> 00:15:00.129
Tim Nunamaker: So now we're allowing for IDs to be different, by default to, like, specifically

102
00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:03.100
Tim Nunamaker: Each application should get its own ID for the data.

103
00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:05.470
Tim Nunamaker: In some cases.

104
00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:14.140
Tim Nunamaker: Owners may have reasons to disclose, like, a universally identifier. For example, this is my, state.

105
00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:17.289
Tim Nunamaker: driver's license DID, this is who I am.

106
00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:19.329
Tim Nunamaker: But that's not the default.

107
00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:28.250
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so the next change here… is…

108
00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:49.159
Tim Nunamaker: there may… this is really to help applications. So if you are a finance application, or a medical application, and you're getting data, and you don't actually know that it's stale, you might end up giving your users bad advice. So these applications should have some way of knowing how up-to-date is the data that you're getting, that I'm getting.

109
00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:51.670
Tim Nunamaker: And… Oh.

110
00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:56.669
Tim Nunamaker: like, this is… I guess one of multiple pieces where

111
00:15:56.790 --> 00:16:00.900
Tim Nunamaker: We just want to make sure that the client application has an accurate picture.

112
00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:07.979
Tim Nunamaker: This is rounded, to the nearest hour in terms of, like, the freshness time.

113
00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:13.760
Tim Nunamaker: Because we think that that is sufficient and doesn't, like, leak too much information.

114
00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:30.459
Tim Nunamaker: And there's another pull request here, number 391, which extends this idea beyond simple data access to what if you're accessing over MCP tools, what if you are doing a search, which is, like, an optional

115
00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:38.980
Tim Nunamaker: you know, extension that you could add onto your PDPP server, then the search results should also express if the data is fresh or if not.

116
00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:40.760
Tim Nunamaker: How stale it is.

117
00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:47.770
Tim Nunamaker: Moving on to the next, change here.

118
00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:55.369
Tim Nunamaker: So, this is really about… Applications that not only read data.

119
00:16:55.480 --> 00:17:12.259
Tim Nunamaker: sort of just-in-time or on-demand, but are syncing data. If there's a use case where there's a lot of data, the application needs to continuously sync it over. There was just a bug fix here where if the… if the application was granted access.

120
00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:20.460
Tim Nunamaker: Starting from some time in the past, they didn't have a cursor that they could pass in order to start syncing from the very beginning.

121
00:17:20.599 --> 00:17:24.499
Tim Nunamaker: And there are some other smaller fixes, like…

122
00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:32.209
Tim Nunamaker: If there's a delay in getting the records, we want to make sure that that actually comes through the next time they sync, and it isn't lost.

123
00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:38.310
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, so probably nothing to… disputable in here.

124
00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:41.060
Art A: Cool.

125
00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:49.649
Tim Nunamaker: The next, change here is related to the discussion we had just a moment ago about

126
00:17:49.900 --> 00:18:06.050
Tim Nunamaker: Evidence, we were talking about it from the standpoint of how do you establish authority of somebody to grant access to their child's data, for example. But this is actually more general. What if you want to establish evidence of the provenance of the data?

127
00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:11.209
Tim Nunamaker: And I think… This came from some community feedback, so thank you for that.

128
00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:22.279
Tim Nunamaker: And… There are specific… mechanisms mentioned in PR number 373, which are a little bit less of a formal

129
00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:36.919
Tim Nunamaker: proposal going straight into the core spec, but there's sketches that sort of map out. These are, like, directionally some choices that could be made about what's the first mechanism that we want to support, and how would that work?

130
00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:38.989
Tim Nunamaker: So I think that's a good…

131
00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:46.480
Tim Nunamaker: top… like, if you're interested in this topic, go take a look at that PR and feel free to leave some comments and have some discussion with us about that.

132
00:18:50.990 --> 00:18:54.970
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so moving on to the next one, I think we've just got a few more here.

133
00:18:55.230 --> 00:19:00.880
Tim Nunamaker: A data source… wants to… Send a user record.

134
00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:02.290
Tim Nunamaker: And…

135
00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:12.719
Tim Nunamaker: maybe, the complete record is not available at the time, so this is another bug fix, basically. We don't want to.

136
00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:18.710
Tim Nunamaker: overwrite Some fields, if they're missing in another update.

137
00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:23.890
Tim Nunamaker: Sorry, this is just a little bit of a technical… issue,

138
00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:27.769
Tim Nunamaker: But I'm having some trouble articulating clearly, but…

139
00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:41.180
Tim Nunamaker: Before, records were basically full snapshots, so when those were transmitted, it could potentially erase some data. So this is just adding a little bit of, resilience to the process of data being collected.

140
00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:49.920
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so… We're thinking ahead a little bit about

141
00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:55.769
Tim Nunamaker: what kinds of roles does a PDPP server play, and what roles could they play in the future?

142
00:19:55.910 --> 00:20:02.460
Tim Nunamaker: And one idea was that data could be written back Into a user's resource server.

143
00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:09.260
Tim Nunamaker: From a client. So that would be a different kind of client than an accessor, and…

144
00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:28.669
Tim Nunamaker: We're not really defining how that would work, but we want to make sure that different kinds of roles and different kinds of tokens can be created in the future. And so, this is just sort of in the theme of creating space for extensions and… and improvements to the protocol.

145
00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:33.580
Tim Nunamaker: New kinds of tokens will be… it will be possible to add those in.

146
00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:47.920
Tim Nunamaker: For privacy reasons, we also decided that client applications should not be allowed to get tokens that don't expire ever, so they're going to have to, use a refresh token.

147
00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:49.689
Tim Nunamaker: to renew that.

148
00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:53.540
Tim Nunamaker: Even if it's a number of days, or…

149
00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:58.660
Tim Nunamaker: you know, months. Eventually, it will have to expire. And this is just a standard practice.

150
00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.799
Tim Nunamaker: I think the others are just sort of simple bug fixes.

151
00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:11.140
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, and the same theme of extensibility.

152
00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:13.700
Tim Nunamaker: We want to be able to.

153
00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:23.899
Tim Nunamaker: support other authorization protocols besides just OAuth. So we discussed this a little bit in some of our September working sessions, and we just

154
00:21:23.950 --> 00:21:35.060
Tim Nunamaker: Shored up how that… how the protocol, expresses its relationship to authorization protocols, so that it's possible to support that in the future.

155
00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:46.189
Tim Nunamaker: There is a section in the core protocol that outlines topics that are still open questions that we may solve for within PDPP, and that's Section 12.

156
00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:48.969
Tim Nunamaker: Through PR377.

157
00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:54.769
Tim Nunamaker: And the collection profile is a separate spec from the core spec.

158
00:21:54.790 --> 00:22:13.290
Tim Nunamaker: Which defines a way for data connectors to basically be plugins into a runtime that can source data into a resource server. And that is now fully owned by the Data Connectors repo, so it hasn't gone away, but it has relocated, and you can find it there.

159
00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:17.980
Tim Nunamaker: Under the PDPP… excuse me, PDP-Connect org in GitHub.

160
00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:24.730
Tim Nunamaker: And we added some diagrams and cleaned up some copy in the spec, so just some quality of life improvements.

161
00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:37.259
Tim Nunamaker: AI training is something that we've been thinking about. Like, what is the right way to model AI training? It's…

162
00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:42.069
Tim Nunamaker: It's a little bit of a special kind of permission, because

163
00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:46.490
Tim Nunamaker: Once your data has trained an AI model, that's…

164
00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:56.520
Tim Nunamaker: that's just out there in the world. Revoking access is different than, like, untraining a model. And so we're still sort of working out exactly

165
00:22:57.110 --> 00:22:59.339
Tim Nunamaker: What the right,

166
00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:07.320
Tim Nunamaker: solution is for AI training, or to what extent AI training needs its own solution, but one…

167
00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:20.100
Tim Nunamaker: One, I would say, more open question that's in draft right now, or at least we'll have a PR landing in the next day or two, is about this question of If a user changes their mind.

168
00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:25.550
Tim Nunamaker: Today, they can revoke the grant. Great. Requests will be denied.

169
00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:33.600
Tim Nunamaker: For data, but what if they are… what if the client application already has the data, and the user changes their mind and revokes the grant?

170
00:23:33.970 --> 00:23:40.320
Tim Nunamaker: can you somehow Have the user opt out of AI training at that point in time.

171
00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:53.480
Tim Nunamaker: There are retention policies in PDPP, but if the retention policy that the user agreed to is 60 days, but 2 days after the grant was created, they want to revoke.

172
00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:59.410
Tim Nunamaker: their consent. There isn't really a solution for that, and if we want a solution.

173
00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:03.719
Tim Nunamaker: One path is we could do what Australia does.

174
00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:10.879
Tim Nunamaker: In their consumer data rights, which is that the server would push a notification to the client saying.

175
00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:13.280
Tim Nunamaker: The user has withdrawn consent.

176
00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:16.110
Tim Nunamaker: But that is a little bit…

177
00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:20.079
Tim Nunamaker: That depends on the client reliably receiving that push.

178
00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:28.529
Tim Nunamaker: So if their server has downtime or something, we can't really guarantee that consent has been withdrawn from the client's perspective.

179
00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:32.610
Tim Nunamaker: Another option is sort of taken from the security

180
00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:36.749
Tim Nunamaker: Domain, which is this idea of provisioning

181
00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:54.359
Tim Nunamaker: leases. So if you have a grant that's good for 6 months to access data. maybe once a day, or every 4 hours, you actually need to go refresh a lease with the server against that grant, so that you can continue to.

182
00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:55.860
Tim Nunamaker: access the data.

183
00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:57.480
Tim Nunamaker: And…

184
00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:06.789
Tim Nunamaker: this could probably be applied to other things besides just AI training, any kind of reason to have consent withdrawn.

185
00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:16.010
Tim Nunamaker: could potentially support this, but this is, I would say, a little bit of a novel application of the lease pattern.

186
00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:32.200
Tim Nunamaker: And so, this is something that would benefit from discussion. It's not 100% for sure whether this would go into V0.1, but this seems like a good opportunity to just invite some discussion, so there should be a pull request up this week if you want to check that out.

187
00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:49.150
Tim Nunamaker: And then, there are some things that we just can't get in, in this version, and a lot of these came from feedback. We've… we've basically looked at all these different topics.

188
00:25:49.210 --> 00:26:05.839
Tim Nunamaker: And made sure that there's nothing in the spec that's blocking them from being solved or addressed later on. In some cases, we've made some minor changes to sort of set up the possibility to solve for subgrants, or changing app ownership, or derivative data.

189
00:26:06.010 --> 00:26:11.240
Tim Nunamaker: And I think they could potentially be fast follows in future versions of the protocol.

190
00:26:11.500 --> 00:26:14.730
Tim Nunamaker: But just wanted to call out that.

191
00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:18.010
Tim Nunamaker: These have all been considered, and…

192
00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:20.760
Tim Nunamaker: Like, will likely be revisited in the future.

193
00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:27.610
Tim Nunamaker: Just to recap a couple of the key points, across all these changes.

194
00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:30.490
Tim Nunamaker: I would say some of the most important ones are.

195
00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:40.190
Tim Nunamaker: With this version of PDPP, the owner really gets to narrow the consent that they give, even if it means that the client application isn't getting what they want.

196
00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:47.919
Tim Nunamaker: Different consent requests can… Involve multiple purposes, and we are…

197
00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:52.379
Tim Nunamaker: Trying to respect the user by creating a different grant for each purpose.

198
00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:54.050
Tim Nunamaker: And then finally.

199
00:26:54.230 --> 00:27:05.179
Tim Nunamaker: For privacy reasons, apps are getting unique identifiers for each individual, so that they can't collude, and they can't try to discover information about users that they shouldn't.

200
00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:25.869
Tim Nunamaker: So, like I said, you can go to the pull request 374 in the PDPP repo, and leave comments, read through these changes, jump into the Discord server. All of that is amazing. Thanks for all the contributions over the open comment period. It's been really…

201
00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:32.970
Tim Nunamaker: Really awesome to see everybody, Coming into this problem and supporting this project.

202
00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:39.429
Tim Nunamaker: And… yeah, watch out in October for the 0.12 lock.

203
00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:40.939
Tim Nunamaker: And I guess…

204
00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:46.939
Art A: Tim, one question that did come up in just a call that I just had immediately before this one was.

205
00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:58.009
Art A: Does the standard set any protections or guardrails, or, like, how does the standard work in relation to once the data is in the recipient application?

206
00:27:59.030 --> 00:28:02.029
Art A: Do we make any guarantees at the…

207
00:28:02.450 --> 00:28:10.939
Art A: resource server or, at the authorization server level that ensures that data is only used for that specific purpose that's defined by the user.

208
00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:16.919
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, I mean, there are physical limits to what the protocol can do there.

209
00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:22.819
Tim Nunamaker: So… Like, for whatever part of it is within the server's control.

210
00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:26.639
Tim Nunamaker: Like, you're making a query for data to the server.

211
00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:28.899
Tim Nunamaker: Of course, the server is going to

212
00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:31.860
Tim Nunamaker: Enforce the rules associated with the grant.

213
00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:35.860
Tim Nunamaker: But for whatever happens in the client.

214
00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:42.750
Tim Nunamaker: The server doesn't have control over that, so the best that we can do is basically make the client

215
00:28:43.540 --> 00:28:51.630
Tim Nunamaker: be explicit about what it's agreeing to, and that comes in in a few different places in PDPP, like…

216
00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:56.790
Tim Nunamaker: The client can and should attach terms and conditions in their privacy policy.

217
00:28:57.190 --> 00:28:59.990
Tim Nunamaker: They should express what the retention.

218
00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:07.570
Tim Nunamaker: policy is for the data, and so if a client says, I'm only going to keep your data for 30 days.

219
00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:10.640
Tim Nunamaker: And they don't, and the user

220
00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:19.390
Tim Nunamaker: wants to litigate against that. Like, I'm not a lawyer, maybe, maybe the fact that it's attributed through the grant is…

221
00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:22.709
Tim Nunamaker: you know, supportive for the user.

222
00:29:23.810 --> 00:29:28.380
Tim Nunamaker: So, unfortunately, yeah, we can't guarantee everything, but we try to

223
00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:32.920
Tim Nunamaker: At least put it all in the contract. You know, defined as the grant.

224
00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:38.200
Art A: And so like if a user is like I don't want my data used for AI training.

225
00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:45.039
Art A: There's no, like, like… Other than that agreement.

226
00:29:45.750 --> 00:29:50.479
Art A: There's no, like, verifiable way for that user to ensure that their data isn't used for AI training.

227
00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:52.900
Tim Nunamaker: Right.

228
00:29:53.010 --> 00:30:01.700
Tim Nunamaker: Like… On a technical level, Maybe there are some specific kinds of integrations that you could set up, like.

229
00:30:02.070 --> 00:30:04.410
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe a server could verify that.

230
00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:14.340
Tim Nunamaker: the data was consumed using some specific program in a TEE that created a proof, but I would say that's pretty advanced. Yeah.

231
00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:18.069
Tim Nunamaker: And so it's pretty hard to guarantee that at this stage.

232
00:30:18.770 --> 00:30:26.780
Art A: Okay, and is there prospects for that in terms of, like, have we drafted the standard around that in… standard in such a way that

233
00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:29.990
Art A: Say, for instance, we wanted to put in, like.

234
00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:37.889
Art A: provenance checks or something like that later on that could be layered in on top of it, or is that, like, not within the scope of this project? Like, how are you thinking about that?

235
00:30:38.230 --> 00:30:40.259
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, definitely.

236
00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:44.370
Tim Nunamaker: I mean, the easy thing… luckily, the first step is pretty easy, it's just…

237
00:30:44.850 --> 00:30:51.569
Tim Nunamaker: If we're gonna do providence checks, we're gonna have to check something, like, some kind of proof, or there needs to be some…

238
00:30:51.940 --> 00:30:57.819
Tim Nunamaker: trace of Providence with some signatures, like, whatever the technical

239
00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:04.559
Tim Nunamaker: you know, shape of that is, doesn't really matter. The protocol is sort of leaving a place for that.

240
00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:16.930
Tim Nunamaker: evidence to get dropped in. And then the harder part can come a little bit later, which is, well, how do you actually verify that? How do you… like, what are the technical mechanisms?

241
00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:18.570
Tim Nunamaker: To get that proof.

242
00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:30.700
Art A: And then, like, I guess there was another point that was raised in the Discord in relation to should we be more opinionated in terms of the data structure to support interoperability between data platforms? Do you want to just comment on that briefly?

243
00:31:31.450 --> 00:31:37.330
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, and this has come up in some of the previous sessions as well. I think…

244
00:31:38.410 --> 00:31:45.650
Tim Nunamaker: And maybe just to sort of reflect, or to recap the idea, it's that data portability has suffered in some

245
00:31:45.990 --> 00:31:53.579
Tim Nunamaker: Small part or large part, due to the fact that nobody speaks the same language in terms of the shape of the data.

246
00:31:53.580 --> 00:31:54.250
Art A: Beautiful.

247
00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:56.949
Tim Nunamaker: Photos from one platform to another.

248
00:31:57.110 --> 00:32:01.600
Tim Nunamaker: The schema might be completely different, so that doesn't just work.

249
00:32:01.910 --> 00:32:09.750
Tim Nunamaker: And… I think… we thought about this a lot, like, should PDPP mandate some sort of

250
00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:15.139
Tim Nunamaker: Common schema for a photo, or for an email, or for… tax return.

251
00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:20.429
Tim Nunamaker: I think that could work for certain kinds of data.

252
00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:27.220
Tim Nunamaker: For certain… Large platforms in particular, but the long tail of

253
00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:32.500
Tim Nunamaker: Unique kinds of data of smaller platforms.

254
00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:47.720
Tim Nunamaker: it just gets harder and harder to standardize all of that. And I think the more that you try, the more you lose some of the nuance of, like, in this platform, you get this other extra data, or it's maybe richer in certain ways. And so…

255
00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:51.849
Tim Nunamaker: That's… that's one reason why we chose not to force

256
00:32:52.100 --> 00:33:04.039
Tim Nunamaker: normalizing the data into a standard. Another is we live in the… in a world where AI is becoming more and more prominent, and AI actually handles sort of arbitrary

257
00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:13.659
Tim Nunamaker: schemas very well, and it's kind of a trivial task for AI to say, take my photos from this platform and reshape them to fit this platform.

258
00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:18.290
Tim Nunamaker: And so if we get adoption with AI first.

259
00:33:18.520 --> 00:33:22.980
Tim Nunamaker: then we can maybe think about how do we take the next step. But.

260
00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:34.110
Tim Nunamaker: There's actually nothing stopping anybody from defining their schemas in terms of schema.org schemas, or some convention. Or maybe…

261
00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:43.910
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe, like, in the market, using common schemas will win certain use cases as data portability finds new life.

262
00:33:44.800 --> 00:33:54.890
Tim Nunamaker: it's just a different kind of digital world that we're in than we were maybe 20 years ago. So, I think it's just a little bit too early to say we have to have

263
00:33:55.300 --> 00:33:59.579
Tim Nunamaker: common schemas. And maybe one last point on this is…

264
00:33:59.940 --> 00:34:08.920
Tim Nunamaker: the Digital Markets Act actually enables gatekeepers to build, sort of, bespoke integrations between platforms.

265
00:34:09.030 --> 00:34:18.969
Tim Nunamaker: And I think that is making it harder to justify using common schemas, because there's already a path around that, so… I think the point

266
00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:24.300
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe to sum up the point here, it's like, how can we… Lower the friction.

267
00:34:24.650 --> 00:34:37.029
Tim Nunamaker: at the supply… on the supply side, the data sources, getting the data out is, like, the hard first step. Okay, now how can we do interesting things with it? I think we can still solve that. It'll just be maybe a second step.

268
00:34:38.719 --> 00:34:46.669
Art A: Okay, cool. Thank you for answering those questions. Those are the ones at the top of my mind. Anything that you would want to reflect on based on any of the changes that you made to the standard?

269
00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:48.440
Tim Nunamaker: Mmm.

270
00:34:54.239 --> 00:35:00.380
Tim Nunamaker: There… there is a lot to learn from other standards that are out there, and I think

271
00:35:01.720 --> 00:35:16.149
Tim Nunamaker: I feel really good about the fact that PDPP, sort of from the beginning, has tried to be composable with what already exists and what people are working on already. There are already places in the spec where

272
00:35:16.340 --> 00:35:19.719
Tim Nunamaker: Other standards that are relatively new could.

273
00:35:19.970 --> 00:35:23.190
Tim Nunamaker: could fit well with PDPP.

274
00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:32.550
Tim Nunamaker: C2PA, I think is, one kind of evidence that could drop in to PDPP. So I think we should continue to

275
00:35:32.690 --> 00:35:35.259
Tim Nunamaker: prioritize composability, I think.

276
00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:38.540
Tim Nunamaker: That just buys us a lot of power in the protocol.

277
00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:44.910
Tim Nunamaker: I think there's also… A lot more scope to…

278
00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:52.329
Tim Nunamaker: what a protocol like this can do and can become, then the core of PDP should solve. So this…

279
00:35:52.670 --> 00:36:10.099
Tim Nunamaker: strategy of keeping the core protocol relatively small, and then allowing for a lot of extensions to hook into it through profiles and whatever other, you know, specs that can layer on top of that. I think that's, like, a good way to architect

280
00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:18.519
Tim Nunamaker: PDPP conformance servers that fit different use cases, and if a profile

281
00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:27.510
Tim Nunamaker: It just wins, like, across every use case. That can later be adopted into the core spec. So, I think…

282
00:36:27.510 --> 00:36:28.400
Art A: Yeah.

283
00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:33.680
Tim Nunamaker: We want to be sort of intentional about where we ossify.

284
00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:38.840
Tim Nunamaker: And where we create flexibility and leave space for growth.

285
00:36:39.110 --> 00:36:45.450
Tim Nunamaker: And I think that's a hard thing to solve for, but… So far, so good, hopefully.

286
00:36:48.810 --> 00:36:50.949
Art A: Okay, perfect. Awesome.

287
00:36:51.680 --> 00:37:02.739
Art A: I think that's it, Tim. Let's, wrap this and give folks time back, but I think this is really great, and so the next steps here are for any further feedback to be rolled in over the next couple weeks, lock the standards, and then we can…

288
00:37:02.740 --> 00:37:19.940
Art A: work on, actually, getting folks to adopt it. If there's anybody who's watching this on YouTube who hasn't already, say that they support on the… go to PDPP.dev and say that they support the PDPP principles, please do so. But thank you so much for your work, Tim, and we will stay in touch with the community.

289
00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:22.320
Tim Nunamaker: All right. Likewise. Thanks, Art.

290
00:37:22.710 --> 00:37:23.810
Art A: Thanks, bye.

291
00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:24.830
Tim Nunamaker: Bye.

